-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+Chosen: whole-visit revisions. Each submission is a complete visit record referencing the revision it was based on. Concurrent edits to the same visit create competing complete revisions. Coordinators resolve conflicts by selecting one revision as current.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+**Submission envelope**
+- `visitId` (client-generated UUID)
+- `baseRevisionId` (ID of revision the worker viewed when editing; null for initial submission)
+- `payload` (complete visit fields: plotId, observationDate, observers, speciesCoverEstimates, notes)
+- `idempotencyKey` (client-generated, stable per submission attempt)
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+**Current-state response**
+Returns the selected current revision with:
+- `revisionId`
+- `visitId`
+- `payload`
+- `authorId`, `receivedAt`
+- `status` (`current` or `superseded`)
+- `unresolved` (boolean; true if competing revisions exist and no coordinator selection)
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+**Unresolved work representation**
+When competing revisions exist:
+- `competingRevisions` list of revision IDs with author, receivedAt, and baseRevisionId
+- `baseRevisionId` of the fork point
+The record store does not combine fields; it preserves each submitted complete visit.
+
+**Resolution operation**
+Coordinator selects one revision ID as current via `resolveVisit(visitId, chosenRevisionId, coordinatorId, note)`. The system records the resolution outcome with coordinator identity and contributing submission IDs, marks other revisions as superseded, and updates the visit's current revision pointer. Resolution is idempotent.
+
+Tablet-sync retains base revision IDs and surfaces pending competing revisions to field workers. Review-export must provide a revision comparison screen and hold visits with unresolved competing revisions out of the CSV export.
+
+Neither approach discards original submissions. Review volume increases compared with field-aware merging, but whole-visit intent is preserved and merge rule maintenance is avoided.
 
 ## Downstream Contract Work
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around the submission envelope with baseRevisionId. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation with revision comparison and exclusion of unresolved visits. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | Preserves submitted visit as a unit, avoids merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits without noticing. Larger review queue accepted over maintaining field-level merge rules. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy selected: whole-visit revisions with coordinator resolution.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Exact shape of revision comparison UI in review-export — design after record-store contract is stable.
+2. Tablet-sync offline queueing for competing revisions — depends on tablet-sync LLD.
